the right person got eliminated.

743
00:32:03,171 --> 00:32:05,506
There were just too many strong players.

744
00:32:05,590 --> 00:32:08,134
<i>I didn't really do much</i>
<i>in today's main match.</i>

745
00:32:08,217 --> 00:32:12,931
<i>So I went to prison,</i>
<i>thinking that was where I belonged.</i>

746
00:32:13,014 --> 00:32:14,557
<i>I had a lot of fun in the game though,</i>

747
00:32:15,141 --> 00:32:16,309
<i>so it was less painful.</i>

748
00:32:16,392 --> 00:32:19,354
<i>But it's a shame</i>
<i>I couldn't do better for Nucksal's sake.</i>

749
00:32:19,437 --> 00:32:21,731
And I was honored
to be eliminated with Jin-ho.

750
00:32:21,814 --> 00:32:23,816
I still can't believe
I'm going home on day one.

751
00:32:24,692 --> 00:32:26,778
<i>The players here</i>

752
00:32:26,861 --> 00:32:30,031
<i>are either former champions</i>
<i>or otherwise accomplished players.</i>

753
00:32:30,114 --> 00:32:33,076
<i>I don't know why I thought</i>
<i>I could afford to take it easy here.</i>

754
00:32:33,660 --> 00:32:37,622
I'm hit with a huge wave of regret,
but I'll just have to accept it.

755
00:32:37,705 --> 00:32:39,207
Still, it's definitely
a hard pill to swallow.

756
00:32:39,290 --> 00:32:43,252
<i>I guess </i>The Devil's Plan Season 3
<i>was just not meant to be my stage.</i>

757
00:32:43,336 --> 00:32:45,463
I'll be watching from afar.

758
00:32:45,546 --> 00:32:47,340
- Good luck.
- Well done.

759
00:32:47,423 --> 00:32:48,549
- Good luck!
- See you guys.

760
00:32:48,633 --> 00:32:49,592
- Well done.
- Sorry.

761
00:32:49,676 --> 00:32:50,551
It's fine. Don't worry.

762
00:32:54,722 --> 00:32:56,140
Man, what a bummer.

763
00:32:56,724 --> 00:32:57,767
Jin-ho.

764
00:33:14,450 --> 00:33:19,372
THE DEVIL'S PLAN: DEAD AGAIN

765
00:33:19,455 --> 00:33:20,707
Jin-ho.

766
00:33:25,753 --> 00:33:27,797
<i>As the player in first place,</i>

767
00:33:27,880 --> 00:33:29,382
<i>Ha Seok-jin will receive four Pieces.</i>

768
00:33:29,465 --> 00:33:30,633
- Congrats.
- Congrats.

769
00:33:32,844 --> 00:33:33,886
Too bad.

770
00:33:33,970 --> 00:33:35,513
I forgot about the Pieces.

771
00:33:36,347 --> 00:33:39,475
- We were too busy surviving.
- Yeah, survival made me forget.

772
00:33:39,559 --> 00:33:41,686
<i>That concludes the prison match.</i>

773
00:33:41,769 --> 00:33:44,480
<i>Players, please return to prison.</i>

774
00:33:44,981 --> 00:33:45,982
Good job, everyone.

775
00:33:46,065 --> 00:33:48,067
- Well played.
- Well done.

776
00:33:48,151 --> 00:33:49,986
There are only a few of us left.

777
00:33:50,069 --> 00:33:52,238
- Well played.
- You too, Hyun-gyu.

778
00:33:52,822 --> 00:33:55,158
Determining when to play
card six was difficult.

779
00:33:55,241 --> 00:33:56,075
Yeah.

780
00:33:56,159 --> 00:33:57,785
I considered playing it early,

781
00:33:58,411 --> 00:34:00,121
but I couldn't find the right turn.

782
00:34:07,712 --> 00:34:08,671
This is nuts.

783
00:34:13,801 --> 00:34:15,720
Look at them, all above us.

784
00:34:15,803 --> 00:34:17,138
How scary.

785
00:34:17,221 --> 00:34:19,682
You guys will feel this fear tomorrow.

786
00:34:20,516 --> 00:34:21,559
Just wait and see.

787
00:34:23,936 --> 00:34:24,896
Seriously.

788
00:34:24,979 --> 00:34:26,564
They're acting like kids.

789
00:34:26,647 --> 00:34:28,066
Having fun?

790
00:34:29,192 --> 00:34:30,193
What was that?

791
00:34:30,276 --> 00:34:31,277
- What?
- "Having fun?"

792
00:34:31,360 --> 00:34:32,570
Oh, that's what he said.

793
00:34:33,738 --> 00:34:35,698
We're massively outnumbered.

794
00:34:35,782 --> 00:34:36,991
- Against them?
- Yeah.

795
00:34:37,075 --> 00:34:38,618
Don't do that.

796
00:34:38,701 --> 00:34:39,660
Don't draw sides.

797
00:34:39,744 --> 00:34:42,163
- Right, but--
- Just leave it at having an upsetting day.

798
00:34:42,246 --> 00:34:43,081
You know,

799
00:34:43,790 --> 00:34:45,917
the moment the Jang-Oh Alliance
was revived…

800
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:48,961
Yeah, they're so tight.

801
00:34:49,045 --> 00:34:52,507
- They have a lot of history.
- Yeah, they do.

802
00:34:52,590 --> 00:34:54,133
It's a tried and tested friendship.

803
00:34:54,217 --> 00:34:55,510
I'll give it to Oh Hyun-min.

804
00:34:56,302 --> 00:34:58,179
- Thank you.
- It's happening again, isn't it?

805
00:34:58,262 --> 00:35:00,223
- <i>The Genius </i>duo.
- I'm so sick of Jang-Oh!

806
00:35:00,306 --> 00:35:04,310
It takes talent to stay in the game
with people you like.

807
00:35:04,393 --> 00:35:06,562
If I had just wanted to survive,

808
00:35:06,646 --> 00:35:08,898
I could've just groveled
at Dong-min's feet.

809
00:35:08,981 --> 00:35:10,525
Then I could've avoided prison.

810
00:35:12,276 --> 00:35:13,986
But that's not fun.

811
00:35:14,487 --> 00:35:18,741
I thought about
who might be favorably disposed

812
00:35:18,825 --> 00:35:20,868
toward me.

813
00:35:20,952 --> 00:35:23,538
Obviously, there's Dong-min and Hyun-min,

814
00:35:24,288 --> 00:35:26,165
<i>both of whom seem to hold</i>
<i>some degree of guilt</i>

815
00:35:26,249 --> 00:35:28,543
<i>and goodwill toward me.</i>

816
00:35:29,127 --> 00:35:30,670
Then, there are my fellow prisoners.

817
00:35:30,753 --> 00:35:33,089
<i>I feel like I can trust them.</i>

818
00:35:33,172 --> 00:35:37,093
<i>They chose greater good</i>
<i>over personal gain to begin with.</i>

819
00:35:37,176 --> 00:35:40,847
So I felt that I could rally
with these people.

820
00:35:40,930 --> 00:35:44,225
And most importantly,
I now have a very clear sense of players

821
00:35:44,308 --> 00:35:46,352
I'd rather not play alongside.

822
00:35:46,435 --> 00:35:48,437
You know… What was his name again?

823
00:35:48,521 --> 00:35:51,190
Hyun Kyung-lyul, was it?

824
00:35:51,274 --> 00:35:54,026
Do any of you know what he's like?

825
00:35:54,110 --> 00:35:55,444
It's my first time meeting him.

826
00:35:55,528 --> 00:35:58,573
During Lay the Grass earlier,

827
00:35:58,656 --> 00:36:00,741
- I noticed that he's quite meticulous.
- Right.

828
00:36:00,825 --> 00:36:05,163
Between Won-young,
Dong-jae, and Kyung-lyul,

829
00:36:05,246 --> 00:36:07,123
I think Kyung-lyul is the most intense.

830
00:36:07,206 --> 00:36:08,499
Personality-wise,

831
00:36:08,583 --> 00:36:11,377
I don't see someone
like Kyung-lyul or Dong-jae

832
00:36:11,460 --> 00:36:14,463
getting along with Dong-min.

833
00:36:14,547 --> 00:36:16,465
That guy over there

834
00:36:17,925 --> 00:36:19,177
wouldn't welcome them.

835
00:36:19,260 --> 00:36:20,845
Those guys have that lone wolf mentality.

836
00:36:20,928 --> 00:36:24,557
They're all commanders who wanna
push forward what they believe is right.

837
00:36:24,640 --> 00:36:28,102
Yurisa, you don't have
anything going on, right?

838
00:36:28,186 --> 00:36:29,103
- No alliances?
- No.

839
00:36:29,187 --> 00:36:30,104
Solo play.

840
00:36:30,605 --> 00:36:33,357
It's the first day,
so I'm just trying to feel people out.

841
00:36:34,192 --> 00:36:37,403
Honestly, those guys
have too strong an alliance.

842
00:36:37,486 --> 00:36:39,071
You mean Dong-min's alliance?

843
00:36:39,155 --> 00:36:40,031
It's always the same.

844
00:36:40,114 --> 00:36:42,617
- He always does that in any game.
- Yeah.

845
00:36:42,700 --> 00:36:46,537
Dong-min is good at getting everyone
on the same page

846
00:36:46,621 --> 00:36:48,623
- and carrying his team.
- It's his way or the highway.

847
00:36:48,706 --> 00:36:53,836
Typically, you scout out and win over
whoever's in the best position.

848
00:36:53,920 --> 00:36:57,465
But even before the game started,
he got Hyun-min to stand on his side.

849
00:36:58,382 --> 00:36:59,634
They need to be separated.

850
00:37:00,176 --> 00:37:02,929
But the issue is,
if you're a weaker player

851
00:37:03,012 --> 00:37:06,265
and the Jang-Oh Alliance
invites you to join them,

852
00:37:06,349 --> 00:37:08,434
you have no choice but to tag along

853
00:37:08,517 --> 00:37:10,895
because it feels like they'd somehow
secure your survival.

854
00:37:10,978 --> 00:37:13,147
- I don't think Kyung-lyul would.
- No, he wouldn't.

855
00:37:13,231 --> 00:37:14,941
- Right?
- They wouldn't click.

856
00:37:15,024 --> 00:37:16,400
That's what I think too.

857
00:37:17,068 --> 00:37:19,987
- He's not the type to take orders.
- I noticed earlier

858
00:37:20,071 --> 00:37:22,448
that people were surprisingly resistant
to being corralled.

859
00:37:22,531 --> 00:37:24,367
- There are no weak players right now.
- Yeah.

860
00:37:24,450 --> 00:37:26,035
- Everyone is a predator.
- Right.

861
00:37:28,329 --> 00:37:30,373
There must be something with the Pieces.

862
00:37:30,915 --> 00:37:32,792
John, you only have one too, right?

863
00:37:32,875 --> 00:37:33,834
Yes.

864
00:37:33,918 --> 00:37:35,419
We don't have enough to try anything.

865
00:37:35,503 --> 00:37:37,672
- You can try it with mine.
- Really?

866
00:37:37,755 --> 00:37:38,589
Thank you.

867
00:37:40,132 --> 00:37:41,300
That wasn't a transfer.

868
00:37:42,885 --> 00:37:44,220
Let me make that clear.

869
00:37:44,303 --> 00:37:46,514
"XITSUH,
please leave this place immediately."

870
00:37:47,181 --> 00:37:49,934
I wonder what purpose the Pieces serve.
There must be something.

871
00:37:54,272 --> 00:37:55,106
Jeez, that scared me.

872
00:37:55,189 --> 00:37:56,774
Lights out at 11.

873
00:37:56,857 --> 00:37:57,858
- Oh, at 11?
- Oh jeez.

874
00:37:57,942 --> 00:38:00,236
- Oh, they dim the lights.
- Yeah.

875
00:38:02,280 --> 00:38:04,115
What now? Do we have to sleep?

876
00:38:04,949 --> 00:38:06,075
Night light?

877
00:38:07,118 --> 00:38:08,786
At least I made it through the first day.

878
00:38:09,954 --> 00:38:12,123
- That alone is a success.
- It sure is.

879
00:38:15,084 --> 00:38:16,294
Who's stomping around?

880
00:38:16,794 --> 00:38:18,504
There's too much noise
coming from upstairs.

881
00:38:19,005 --> 00:38:22,300
- We'll have 12 players tomorrow, right?
- Yeah.

882
00:38:22,383 --> 00:38:25,052
If two get eliminated
in the main match tomorrow,

883
00:38:25,761 --> 00:38:27,179
this competition won't work.

884
00:38:27,263 --> 00:38:29,390
- That's what I think.
- Yeah, they won't eliminate two.

885
00:38:29,473 --> 00:38:31,434
Today's eliminations
might've been necessary,

886
00:38:31,517 --> 00:38:33,311
- but they'll eliminate only one tomorrow.
- Agreed.

887
00:38:33,394 --> 00:38:34,770
So many left on the first day.

888
00:38:34,854 --> 00:38:35,855
That being said,

889
00:38:35,938 --> 00:38:38,524
when you're at 12 players or so,

890
00:38:38,607 --> 00:38:41,527
- an alliance of five is the most solid.
- Yeah.

891
00:38:41,610 --> 00:38:45,281
And I believe
if you're gonna form an alliance,

892
00:38:45,364 --> 00:38:47,867
there has to be something in it
for everyone.

893
00:38:47,950 --> 00:38:49,618
We'll be doing something again tomorrow,

894
00:38:50,244 --> 00:38:51,329
and I think

895
00:38:52,163 --> 00:38:56,334
it'd be good if we could create
a game plan beforehand.

896
00:38:56,417 --> 00:38:59,211
We obviously don't know
what tomorrow's game will be.

897
00:38:59,295 --> 00:39:01,756
But at this point in the past seasons,

898
00:39:01,839 --> 00:39:05,760
the players in the living area
and the players in prison started to bond,

899
00:39:05,843 --> 00:39:09,263
and their alliances solidified.

900
00:39:09,972 --> 00:39:13,100
But I think it's gonna be
a bit different in Season 3.

901
00:39:13,184 --> 00:39:18,356
<i>I'm sure quite a few people</i>
<i>will have a chat tonight.</i>

902
00:39:19,065 --> 00:39:19,899
Hyun-min.

903
00:39:19,982 --> 00:39:20,816
Yeah?

904
00:39:27,823 --> 00:39:29,492
Who do you wanna look out for?

905
00:39:29,992 --> 00:39:31,410
- If I were to choose just one?
- Yeah.

906
00:39:31,494 --> 00:39:32,453
XITSUH.

907
00:39:32,536 --> 00:39:34,789
- Yeah, me too.
- Right.

908
00:39:34,872 --> 00:39:37,666
- You, me, and XITSUH.
- Right.

909
00:39:38,250 --> 00:39:39,502
What did you think about Dong-jae?

910
00:39:39,585 --> 00:39:42,421
To be honest,
I find him a little difficult.

911
00:39:42,505 --> 00:39:46,175
His individual skills aren't bad.
He's a pretty good player.

912
00:39:46,258 --> 00:39:49,387
But both Dong-jae and XITSUH

913
00:39:50,096 --> 00:39:53,099
can be a bit impulsive.

914
00:39:53,182 --> 00:39:55,434
There are times
they're a little hard to rein in.

915
00:39:55,518 --> 00:39:59,605
The moment they fixate on something,
they just barrel toward it.

916
00:39:59,688 --> 00:40:01,899
If they can't control their impulses,

917
00:40:01,982 --> 00:40:04,026
- then it's over from there.
- Exactly.

918
00:40:04,110 --> 00:40:06,195
But I wouldn't mind giving him a shot.

919
00:40:06,278 --> 00:40:09,156
I like Dong-jae too.

920
00:40:09,240 --> 00:40:11,617
But he and XITSUH share certain qualities.

921
00:40:11,700 --> 00:40:13,411
I told you that's just their style.

922
00:40:13,494 --> 00:40:15,454
Right. My point is,

923
00:40:15,538 --> 00:40:18,707
if we can keep them under control,
then we'll be fine.

924
00:40:21,335 --> 00:40:22,795
Nobody is sleeping yet, right?

925
00:40:22,878 --> 00:40:24,880
- I'm gonna use the hair dryer real quick.
- Go ahead.

926
00:40:36,892 --> 00:40:37,893
Wait.

927
00:40:43,941 --> 00:40:44,984
It's not going in.

928
00:40:53,909 --> 00:40:55,953
- Huh? The wiring isn't connected.
- Hush, hush.

929
00:41:04,420 --> 00:41:06,130
I don't think the wiring is set up.

930
00:41:06,213 --> 00:41:07,423
Is there no electricity?

931
00:41:14,013 --> 00:41:16,348
I think there's something here.

932
00:41:22,146 --> 00:41:23,063
Hold on, what's this?

933
00:41:23,647 --> 00:41:24,523
Kyung-lyul.

934
00:41:25,149 --> 00:41:26,442
Kyung-lyul.

935
00:41:27,526 --> 00:41:28,402
This one's also…

936
00:41:46,879 --> 00:41:47,755
Hang on.

937
00:41:50,341 --> 00:41:52,092
As I said,
if anyone finds anything, it's me.

938
00:41:52,176 --> 00:41:55,387
<i>It looked like there was a dryer</i>
<i>at each station,</i>

939
00:41:55,471 --> 00:41:58,849
so I assumed the power outlet would work,
but I couldn't plug it in.

940
00:41:58,933 --> 00:42:01,435
So I took a closer look
and noticed that it was blocked.

941
00:42:01,519 --> 00:42:03,395
I lifted it to check it out.

942
00:42:03,479 --> 00:42:05,272
<i>At first, I didn't think much of it.</i>

943
00:42:05,356 --> 00:42:06,732
<i>But when I looked at it closely,</i>

944
00:42:06,815 --> 00:42:08,567
it almost looked like a speaker.

945
00:42:09,527 --> 00:42:11,612
<i>It looked like</i>
<i>a Piece would fit into it perfectly.</i>

946
00:42:11,695 --> 00:42:14,782
I tried pushing the bar,
and it went right up.

947
00:42:16,325 --> 00:42:17,409
And then it hit me.

948
00:42:24,458 --> 00:42:25,417
Then,

949
00:42:26,418 --> 00:42:28,087
what's the team you're picturing?

950
00:42:33,050 --> 00:42:35,052
Shindong doesn't have
any of those qualities.

951
00:42:35,636 --> 00:42:38,305
He would never go off
on his own like them.

952
00:42:38,389 --> 00:42:39,848
He's savvy as well.

953
00:42:39,932 --> 00:42:41,475
I think he'd be a good fit.

954
00:42:41,559 --> 00:42:43,435
Maybe everyone needs to insert one.

955
00:42:44,228 --> 00:42:48,399
Maybe everyone here needs to insert one
for something to be activated.

956
00:42:48,482 --> 00:42:51,026
- That doesn't add up.
- Maybe it's a teamwork thing.

957
00:42:51,110 --> 00:42:53,862
But by this time tomorrow,
there'll be one less person.

958
00:42:53,946 --> 00:42:56,699
But by tomorrow,
there will be people with two Pieces.

959
00:42:56,782 --> 00:42:59,243
When else could we tackle it if not now?

960
00:42:59,326 --> 00:43:02,121
- So tomorrow--
- We wouldn't be able to then.

961
00:43:02,204 --> 00:43:04,999
- Not during the day when everyone's awake.
- Why don't we try it now?

962
00:43:05,082 --> 00:43:06,792
I think now's the time.

963
00:43:07,543 --> 00:43:08,460
Shindong.

964
00:43:09,211 --> 00:43:10,045
Shindong-ding-dong.

965
00:43:10,129 --> 00:43:11,171
Yes?

966
00:43:11,255 --> 00:43:12,881
Can we talk for a second?

967
00:43:12,965 --> 00:43:15,301
- I feel so much better after that shower.
- Yeah.

968
00:43:16,218 --> 00:43:17,177
How was today?

969
00:43:17,803 --> 00:43:20,347
Honestly, it was really different
from what I'd imagined,

970
00:43:20,431 --> 00:43:22,099
so I really had a tough time at the start.

971
00:43:22,182 --> 00:43:24,184
- Right.
- It's like…

972
00:43:24,268 --> 00:43:26,895
<i>After we discovered the mechanism,</i>
<i>I didn't wanna share it.</i>

973
00:43:26,979 --> 00:43:30,733
My first objective has been
to get Jin-ho and Dong-min eliminated.

974
00:43:30,816 --> 00:43:34,278
If this advantage were gonna be divided
among the players in the living area,

975
00:43:34,361 --> 00:43:36,071
<i>I didn't wanna share it with Dong-min,</i>

976
00:43:36,155 --> 00:43:38,532
<i>so I wanted to figure it out by myself</i>
<i>if I could.</i>

977
00:43:38,616 --> 00:43:41,952
- A game like today's was really unusual.
- I see.

978
00:43:42,036 --> 00:43:42,870
Tomorrow,

979
00:43:43,662 --> 00:43:46,790
I expect it'll go back
to a more typical game.

980
00:43:46,874 --> 00:43:48,751
A solid alliance.

981
00:43:49,376 --> 00:43:50,878
- That's the force
- Yeah, that's everything.

982
00:43:50,961 --> 00:43:55,507
that lets you navigate the games
most easily.

983
00:43:55,591 --> 00:43:56,884
- For sure. You're right.
- Yeah.

984
00:43:57,551 --> 00:44:01,055
In games where you need to
gather information,

985
00:44:01,138 --> 00:44:04,016
it gets difficult
if your alliance is too small.

986
00:44:04,600 --> 00:44:07,853
But the issue
with having six people in an alliance

987
00:44:08,520 --> 00:44:11,190
is that one person has to go to prison.

988
00:44:11,273 --> 00:44:13,817
Of course, we should decide
once we see what tomorrow's game is,

989
00:44:13,901 --> 00:44:16,445
but strategy-wise,

990
00:44:17,321 --> 00:44:19,031
I'd like you, me,

991
00:44:20,449 --> 00:44:22,618
and Hyun-min

992
00:44:23,702 --> 00:44:25,162
- to be on the same team.
- Okay.

993
00:44:25,287 --> 00:44:26,288
One more thing.

994
00:44:26,372 --> 00:44:28,082
I want you to find an ally.

995
00:44:28,791 --> 00:44:32,711
It doesn't have to be someone
who's gonna stay with us until the end,

996
00:44:32,795 --> 00:44:36,382
but someone we can work with.

997
00:44:36,465 --> 00:44:40,219
That way, when the time comes
for us to rally together as a team,

998
00:44:40,302 --> 00:44:42,388
we can pull at least six people.

999
00:44:42,471 --> 00:44:44,556
- Six, including you?
- Yeah, exactly.

1000
00:44:44,640 --> 00:44:47,226
So you want me to bring someone
to make six? Got it.

1001
00:44:47,309 --> 00:44:51,438
<i>It's clear the Jang-Oh Alliance is solid.</i>

1002
00:44:51,522 --> 00:44:56,276
And if that's the case, then alliances
within this group are gonna be crucial.

1003
00:45:09,373 --> 00:45:11,333
I'm available anytime,
so if there's a one-on-one…

1004
00:45:11,417 --> 00:45:13,836
No, if there's a game we play in pairs,
let me know.

1005
00:45:13,919 --> 00:45:14,753
In pairs?

1006
00:45:14,837 --> 00:45:16,880
Sure, let's see.

1007
00:45:16,964 --> 00:45:17,840
Did you wash up?

1008
00:45:17,923 --> 00:45:19,216
- No, I'm about to.
- Oh, okay.

1009
00:45:23,387 --> 00:45:25,347
So Kyung-lyul said

1010
00:45:25,431 --> 00:45:28,976
if there's gonna be a game tonight anyway,
we should just let the others know.

1011
00:45:29,977 --> 00:45:32,813
But I'd feel better with some guarantee
that you'd get something out of this.

1012
00:45:35,399 --> 00:45:36,984
Otherwise, it'd feel like such a waste.

1013
00:45:38,444 --> 00:45:39,319
Hold on.

1014
00:46:07,723 --> 00:46:09,516
Hey, look.

1015
00:46:10,350 --> 00:46:11,393
What is this?

1016
00:46:16,940 --> 00:46:18,066
What is this?

1017
00:46:22,237 --> 00:46:23,238
What is this?

1018
00:46:25,115 --> 00:46:26,033
What in the world?

1019
00:46:44,426 --> 00:46:46,303
- Let's take it out first.
- Yeah, okay.

1020
00:46:46,386 --> 00:46:47,721
I'll take it out.

1021
00:46:48,639 --> 00:46:49,848
Goodness me.

1022
00:46:50,974 --> 00:46:52,392
- Should I take it out?
- Yeah, take it out.

1023
00:46:52,476 --> 00:46:53,519
Okay.

1024
00:46:54,019 --> 00:46:55,062
Take it out first.

1025
00:47:03,821 --> 00:47:04,863
What is this?

1026
00:47:05,489 --> 00:47:07,783
What could this be?

1027
00:47:11,745 --> 00:47:13,038
Maybe we insert a Piece.

1028
00:47:13,121 --> 00:47:16,500
When Yi Do came to you
and asked for your help earlier,

1029
00:47:16,583 --> 00:47:19,878
you just flat-out refused.

1030
00:47:19,962 --> 00:47:21,755
- Oh, for the Pieces?
- Yeah.

1031
00:47:21,839 --> 00:47:23,674
That just made no sense.

1032
00:47:23,757 --> 00:47:26,677
- It was downright nonsense.
- Yeah, it was, honestly.

1033
00:47:26,760 --> 00:47:27,970
Try inserting a Piece.

1034
00:47:32,558 --> 00:47:33,809
Does it fit?

1035
00:47:35,143 --> 00:47:37,563
If you hold it upright, it fits.

1036
00:47:37,646 --> 00:47:39,481
But then you'd need about six of them.

1037
00:47:39,565 --> 00:47:40,649
Six?

1038
00:47:41,817 --> 00:47:42,734
Hold on.

1039
00:47:43,485 --> 00:47:44,444
Then…

1040
00:47:45,612 --> 00:47:49,032
<i>We tried inserting a Piece,</i>
<i>and it fit vertically.</i>

1041
00:47:49,116 --> 00:47:51,326
When we counted the slots,

1042
00:47:51,410 --> 00:47:53,579
we realized six would fit vertically.

1043
00:47:54,204 --> 00:47:58,417
But then we'd only have vertical slots,
not horizontal ones.

1044
00:47:58,500 --> 00:48:01,044
Why does it look like that?

1045
00:48:01,128 --> 00:48:03,380
Do you think we could gather six?

1046
00:48:03,463 --> 00:48:04,506
You have two, right?

1047
00:48:04,590 --> 00:48:05,924
I have one. You too, right?

1048
00:48:06,008 --> 00:48:07,676
We're such underdogs.

1049
00:48:07,759 --> 00:48:09,386
Damn it.

1050
00:48:10,929 --> 00:48:12,639
If you find yourself

1051
00:48:13,891 --> 00:48:16,101
a bit stuck,

1052
00:48:16,184 --> 00:48:17,144
just ask for help.

1053
00:48:17,227 --> 00:48:20,272
Then I'll gladly give you advice.

1054
00:48:23,066 --> 00:48:24,651
Is anyone taking a shower?

1055
00:48:24,735 --> 00:48:26,612
I left something in there.

1056
00:48:26,695 --> 00:48:27,905
Are you gonna shower?

1057
00:48:27,988 --> 00:48:29,948
- No, I just wanna pick my stuff up.
- Oh, okay.

1058
00:48:30,032 --> 00:48:31,867
- You're good to go.
- No one's in there, right?

1059
00:48:31,950 --> 00:48:33,368
We'll talk to him when he comes out.

1060
00:48:33,452 --> 00:48:35,829
Okay, we'll tell him the location.

1061
00:48:38,749 --> 00:48:39,875
- We found it.
- What?

1062
00:48:39,958 --> 00:48:42,169
- We found it.
- You did? How?

1063
00:48:43,003 --> 00:48:44,880
Where is it?
Do we need to get everyone involved?

1064
00:48:44,963 --> 00:48:46,590
No, I don't think so.

1065
00:48:46,673 --> 00:48:49,134
We found something on that wall
behind the towel.

1066
00:48:49,635 --> 00:48:51,053
It's over here.

1067
00:48:51,136 --> 00:48:52,554
When we opened it,

1068
00:48:52,638 --> 00:48:54,806
there was some sort of slot,

1069
00:48:55,432 --> 00:48:57,351
and all we have right now are Pieces.

1070
00:48:57,434 --> 00:49:01,021
It's like a grid of skewers.
Six slots, each way.

1071
00:49:01,104 --> 00:49:04,900
It almost looks like a 12-pin connector,
but it's a square.

1072
00:49:04,983 --> 00:49:07,611
- Should we use our Pieces?
- They do fit.

1073
00:49:07,694 --> 00:49:12,699
- They do?
- If you insert it like this, one fits.

1074
00:49:13,575 --> 00:49:16,078
But if you wanna fill all the holes,

1075
00:49:16,161 --> 00:49:18,038
you'd need six this way
and six this way.

1076
00:49:18,121 --> 00:49:19,456
You need to insert it like this.

1077
00:49:19,539 --> 00:49:22,542
- So six of them like that?
- You can fit six this way.

1078
00:49:22,626 --> 00:49:24,336
That makes sense.

1079
00:49:24,419 --> 00:49:28,298
There are grooves
on both sides of this thing,

1080
00:49:28,382 --> 00:49:29,633
so I'm thinking

1081
00:49:30,384 --> 00:49:33,011
we might need to link them up like this.

1082
00:49:33,095 --> 00:49:34,721
I'm thinking maybe a set
goes in like this.

1083
00:49:34,805 --> 00:49:36,056
- That makes sense.
- Yeah.

1084
00:49:36,139 --> 00:49:39,768
Then we'd need…
One, two, three, four, five, six.

1085
00:49:39,851 --> 00:49:41,895
We need 6 this way, 6 that way,
which makes 12 in total.

1086
00:49:41,979 --> 00:49:43,480
- Twelve to fill it up.
- Right.

1087
00:49:43,981 --> 00:49:45,816
Don't we have 12 Pieces if we pool them?

1088
00:49:45,899 --> 00:49:47,067
Yeah, definitely.

1089
00:49:47,150 --> 00:49:50,112
But will people do that
just for one person's benefit?

1090
00:49:50,195 --> 00:49:52,072
If only to open the door, sure.

1091
00:49:53,699 --> 00:49:55,534
But if we don't do it tonight…

1092
00:49:55,617 --> 00:49:58,704
I mean, if this trio isn't present,
it'd be pointless.

1093
00:49:58,787 --> 00:50:01,373
- Then why not just share it with everyone?
- I agree.

1094
00:50:01,456 --> 00:50:04,584
But when we do, we can make a suggestion.

1095
00:50:04,668 --> 00:50:05,919
Since we found it first,

1096
00:50:06,003 --> 00:50:08,588
- the right to enter should be ours first.
- Right.

1097
00:50:08,672 --> 00:50:09,756
To be honest,

1098
00:50:10,632 --> 00:50:12,092
I don't wanna share

1099
00:50:12,175 --> 00:50:14,344
because I don't want Dong-min
to find out about it.

1100
00:50:14,428 --> 00:50:18,557
But it's unlikely
he'll be in prison often.

1101
00:50:18,640 --> 00:50:20,809
- So you wanna share with everyone?
- I think that's better.

1102
00:50:20,892 --> 00:50:22,728
- I think we should.
- Me too.

1103
00:50:22,811 --> 00:50:24,021
- Yeah.
- Okay, let's go.

1104
00:50:25,022 --> 00:50:27,941
Can we all gather for a quick chat?

1105
00:50:28,025 --> 00:50:29,526
- Everyone?
- We've got important news.

1106
00:50:29,609 --> 00:50:31,570
Can we have a group discussion?

1107
00:50:31,653 --> 00:50:32,988
- Sure, of course.
- Come here.

1108
00:50:34,698 --> 00:50:35,532
Okay.

1109
00:50:36,074 --> 00:50:38,577
- We think we found something important.
- Okay.

1110
00:50:38,660 --> 00:50:40,037
But I don't think

1111
00:50:40,787 --> 00:50:42,414
- we can crack it on our own.
- Okay.

1112
00:50:42,497 --> 00:50:45,751
So I think it'd be best to share this

1113
00:50:45,834 --> 00:50:49,755
with everyone in the living area today.

1114
00:50:49,838 --> 00:50:52,549
I was the one who discovered it,

1115
00:50:52,632 --> 00:50:56,803
but these two also found out,
and the three of us tried to work it out.

1116
00:50:56,887 --> 00:50:58,221
But we weren't able to solve it.

1117
00:50:58,305 --> 00:50:59,890
- Okay.
- I mean, this place is so open.

1118
00:50:59,973 --> 00:51:03,351
So I'd like to discuss it
and tackle it together.

1119
00:51:03,435 --> 00:51:05,270
We don't know what's waiting for us.

1120
00:51:05,353 --> 00:51:08,523
But since we're the ones

1121
00:51:09,149 --> 00:51:10,734
who discovered and shared it,

1122
00:51:10,817 --> 00:51:13,153
if it's a solo game,

1123
00:51:13,236 --> 00:51:15,906
- we'd like priority.
- Yeah, of course.

1124
00:51:15,989 --> 00:51:17,783
Absolutely.

1125
00:51:17,866 --> 00:51:20,327
- I'm never interested in the side dishes.
- Right.

1126
00:51:20,410 --> 00:51:22,829
- "Side dishes."
- If I can be of help, I will be.

1127
00:51:22,913 --> 00:51:24,915
I'm speaking for myself.

1128
00:51:24,998 --> 00:51:30,045
We might need to use just one Piece
or twelve Pieces.

1129
00:51:30,128 --> 00:51:31,713
- From what we gathered,
- Really?

1130
00:51:31,797 --> 00:51:34,382
it could take anything
from one to twelve Pieces.

1131
00:51:34,466 --> 00:51:36,009
We need to pool everyone's.

1132
00:51:36,093 --> 00:51:38,220
Everyone needs to chip in
if we wanna do it now.

1133
00:51:38,303 --> 00:51:39,888
How about we all take a look?

1134
00:51:39,971 --> 00:51:41,973
I'm fine with all the other stuff,

1135
00:51:42,057 --> 00:51:44,267
but I'm not sure about
putting up 12 Pieces.

1136
00:51:44,351 --> 00:51:45,769
Right. We won't push for that.

1137
00:51:45,852 --> 00:51:48,396
- That should be our choice.
- We won't, but we don't know how many yet.

1138
00:51:48,480 --> 00:51:51,233
In Season 2,
all the Pieces were compensated,

1139
00:51:51,316 --> 00:51:52,984
so I doubt they'll be used up this time.

1140
00:51:53,068 --> 00:51:54,528
They'll probably give it back to us.

1141
00:51:54,611 --> 00:51:56,238
That's my assumption.

1142
00:51:56,321 --> 00:51:58,865
So we'll share
if we can all agree on that.

1143
00:51:58,949 --> 00:51:59,908
Okay, let's take a look.

1144
00:52:02,452 --> 00:52:04,079
I was trying to use the hair dryer,

1145
00:52:04,162 --> 00:52:06,498
- but the plug wouldn't go in.
- Okay.

1146
00:52:07,290 --> 00:52:08,291
So when I pulled it out…

1147
00:52:08,375 --> 00:52:10,418
We discovered this.

1148
00:52:10,502 --> 00:52:12,963
When you insert a Piece in there,
the next step appears.

1149
00:52:13,046 --> 00:52:14,381
Plugging it in triggers something.

1150
00:52:14,464 --> 00:52:15,423
Look there.

1151
00:52:17,968 --> 00:52:20,220
This was lit up earlier too,
but no one noticed.

1152
00:52:20,303 --> 00:52:21,596
- Not at all.
- Yeah.

1153
00:52:21,680 --> 00:52:22,889
- Then…
- Here.

1154
00:52:22,973 --> 00:52:24,933
- Here.
- Hold on.

1155
00:52:25,976 --> 00:52:27,477
This is it.

1156
00:52:27,561 --> 00:52:28,854
This is what we got up to.

1157
00:52:28,937 --> 00:52:29,813
- Shall we try?
- Okay.

1158
00:52:29,896 --> 00:52:31,231
- I think we can pool 12 Pieces.
- Yeah.

1159
00:52:31,314 --> 00:52:32,399
I have one.

1160
00:52:32,482 --> 00:52:33,358
I have two.

1161
00:52:33,441 --> 00:52:35,152
- I have one.
- I have two.

1162
00:52:35,235 --> 00:52:36,319
How many more do you need?

1163
00:52:36,403 --> 00:52:37,445
I have five.

1164
00:52:37,529 --> 00:52:39,698
If you give us four, we'll be set to go.

1165
00:52:39,781 --> 00:52:41,324
Here's four.

1166
00:52:42,909 --> 00:52:46,163
But before we insert them,
we need to assemble 12 Pieces first.

1167
00:52:46,246 --> 00:52:47,539
- Right.
- Let's do that first.

1168
00:52:47,622 --> 00:52:49,499
- We can see if the grid pattern works.
- Yeah.

1169
00:52:49,583 --> 00:52:51,376
Who wants to try assembling it?

1170
00:52:51,459 --> 00:52:53,378
- The escape room specialist.
- Okay.

1171
00:52:53,461 --> 00:52:55,338
I think we need to stack them
from the bottom.

1172
00:52:56,089 --> 00:52:58,842
- All the Pieces are the same, right?
- Yeah, they are.

1173
00:52:58,925 --> 00:53:00,594
But they're not symmetrical.

1174
00:53:00,677 --> 00:53:02,470
- When flipped, they don't match.
- That changes it.

1175
00:53:02,554 --> 00:53:05,807
The length of the ridges differs.

1176
00:53:05,891 --> 00:53:07,392
- Oh, really?
- Two here, and three here.

1177
00:53:08,143 --> 00:53:09,436
So the issue is,

1178
00:53:09,936 --> 00:53:11,438
it has two orientations.

1179
00:53:11,521 --> 00:53:13,190
If it doesn't work,
you have to take one out.

1180
00:53:13,273 --> 00:53:15,108
- To go in reverse.
- But you don't know which one.

1181
00:53:17,110 --> 00:53:18,862
But that doesn't fit.

1182
00:53:19,571 --> 00:53:22,574
- If it doesn't work, then…
- One side won't fit.

1183
00:53:22,657 --> 00:53:23,825
Wait, this might work.

1184
00:53:24,451 --> 00:53:26,244
No, it doesn't. Wrong way.

1185
00:53:26,953 --> 00:53:28,038
Yeah, wrong way.

1186
00:53:29,372 --> 00:53:30,999
- It's wrong.
- Really?

1187
00:53:33,168 --> 00:53:34,794
This doesn't fit, does it?

1188
00:53:34,878 --> 00:53:36,004
This doesn't?

1189
00:53:36,087 --> 00:53:37,589
It doesn't fit here.

1190
00:53:37,672 --> 00:53:39,424
- It sticks out.
- Right.

1191
00:53:39,507 --> 00:53:42,010
- Maybe we need to alternate the order.
- That's my guess too.

1192
00:53:42,093 --> 00:53:45,138
Instead of placing them all parallel,
try weaving them together like this.

1193
00:53:45,222 --> 00:53:47,390
- Try alternating the order.
- Maybe that's for the better.

1194
00:53:47,474 --> 00:53:49,643
I think that's our only option.

1195
00:53:50,936 --> 00:53:52,437
This is really hard.

1196
00:53:56,441 --> 00:53:57,692
Practice time.

1197
00:53:59,945 --> 00:54:01,988
We couldn't have done this on our own.

1198
00:54:02,072 --> 00:54:04,574
- It's just impossible.
- We couldn't have done it.

1199
00:54:04,658 --> 00:54:08,286
I mean, you can't even attempt this alone.

1200
00:54:08,370 --> 00:54:09,829
But now, the whole group knows.

1201
00:54:10,497 --> 00:54:14,417
At the very least, we know that we need to
practice assembling 12 Pieces.

1202
00:54:16,503 --> 00:54:18,588
We might need it for something else.

1203
00:54:23,009 --> 00:54:24,719
We might not solve it tonight.

1204
00:54:25,512 --> 00:54:28,056
- Once both ends are blocked, that's it.
- How does this look?

1205
00:54:28,139 --> 00:54:30,600
You normally get stuck
at the eighth Piece.

1206
00:54:30,684 --> 00:54:31,768
Really?

1207
00:54:31,851 --> 00:54:33,812
Even after all the progress,

1208
00:54:33,895 --> 00:54:35,480
- once you're stuck…
- Wait.

1209
00:54:35,563 --> 00:54:37,232
It's working.

1210
00:54:37,315 --> 00:54:38,858
That's pretty clever.

1211
00:54:39,609 --> 00:54:43,738
- You just have to go around to insert it.
- Let's do it that way.

1212
00:54:43,822 --> 00:54:46,241
But how will you fit the last one in?

1213
00:54:46,324 --> 00:54:47,951
Maybe that's why it's loose.

1214
00:54:48,034 --> 00:54:49,953
There's a little bit of room.

1215
00:54:50,036 --> 00:54:51,037
They don't click in.

1216
00:54:51,121 --> 00:54:53,581
- You can lift them slightly.
- Yeah, exactly.

1217
00:54:53,665 --> 00:54:56,626
So you can use that space
to insert more.

1218
00:54:56,710 --> 00:55:00,380
You can make space
without taking them apart.

1219
00:55:00,463 --> 00:55:01,423
Is this right?

1220
00:55:01,506 --> 00:55:03,049
You need to lift this too.

1221
00:55:03,133 --> 00:55:04,301
It's blocked on top.

1222
00:55:04,384 --> 00:55:06,761
I see. Lift this one too, right?

1223
00:55:06,845 --> 00:55:09,264
Maybe just go around and lift this one.

1224
00:55:09,347 --> 00:55:11,099
- This is…
- Is it easier to lift two?

1225
00:55:12,684 --> 00:55:14,311
- From the bottom.
- It fits through here.

1226
00:55:14,394 --> 00:55:16,438
- Who knew that would fit?
- Yeah, it does.

1227
00:55:16,521 --> 00:55:18,231
This is how you do it.

1228
00:55:18,315 --> 00:55:20,025
- I got it.
- That's beautiful.

1229
00:55:20,108 --> 00:55:21,359
You should do this part now.

1230
00:55:21,443 --> 00:55:22,861
Just lift the ones
that are blocked on top.

1231
00:55:22,944 --> 00:55:24,612
- The ones that are blocked?
- Yeah, that's it.

1232
00:55:24,696 --> 00:55:27,032
- How does that one fit?
- Finally.

1233
00:55:27,115 --> 00:55:28,742
- This should do it, right?
- I can't see.

1234
00:55:28,825 --> 00:55:29,701
This is the only open option.

1235
00:55:29,784 --> 00:55:30,869
- Here.
- Good.

1236
00:55:30,952 --> 00:55:32,620
Yeah, like that.

1237
00:55:33,997 --> 00:55:34,831
Let's do this part now.

1238
00:55:34,914 --> 00:55:35,832
That's crazy.

1239
00:55:35,915 --> 00:55:37,584
- Like this.
- Yeah, that's it.

1240
00:55:38,668 --> 00:55:40,712
You definitely need help.

1241
00:55:44,257 --> 00:55:46,092
- Nice.
- This is insane.

1242
00:55:46,176 --> 00:55:48,136
- It's beautiful.
- This is so fun.

1243
00:55:48,219 --> 00:55:49,346
- Is that the last one?
- It's done.

1244
00:55:49,429 --> 00:55:51,097
- Yeah, that's the last one.
- The last one.

1245
00:55:51,181 --> 00:55:54,642
- The last one. Let's finish it together.
- Man, this puzzle is beautiful.

1246
00:55:54,726 --> 00:55:56,519
It fits in so beautifully.

1247
00:55:56,603 --> 00:55:59,356
- Ta-da.
- You just need to let it slide down.

1248
00:56:02,067 --> 00:56:04,194
- Let's try inserting it.
- Let's take a look first.

1249
00:56:05,320 --> 00:56:06,488
- Dong-min.
- How glorious.

1250
00:56:06,571 --> 00:56:07,655
We did it.

1251
00:56:07,739 --> 00:56:09,908
Can I insert this since I assembled it?

1252
00:56:09,991 --> 00:56:12,160
- Sure.
- I want the honor of inserting it.

1253
00:56:12,243 --> 00:56:13,370
Knock yourself out.

1254
00:56:13,453 --> 00:56:14,704
That's just insane.

1255
00:56:15,830 --> 00:56:17,123
- Thank you.
- Is this Piece mine?

1256
00:56:18,166 --> 00:56:20,043
Can you shine the light for him?

1257
00:56:22,128 --> 00:56:22,962
There we go.

1258
00:56:24,756 --> 00:56:25,673
It fits.

1259
00:56:25,757 --> 00:56:26,966
It works.

1260
00:56:27,050 --> 00:56:27,967
It fits.

1261
00:56:28,051 --> 00:56:29,636
It fits perfectly.

1262
00:56:30,220 --> 00:56:31,304
Is it in?

1263
00:56:33,348 --> 00:56:34,265
Oh jeez!

1264
00:56:34,349 --> 00:56:36,101
- Look down there.
- Right there.

1265
00:56:38,728 --> 00:56:40,939
Holy crap, there was a secret elevator.

1266
00:56:41,940 --> 00:56:43,691
Don't be too loud.

1267
00:56:45,693 --> 00:56:46,694
You can hear everything.

1268
00:56:46,778 --> 00:56:48,196
Hey, you can't go down.

1269
00:56:51,533 --> 00:56:53,159
- What the heck is this?
- What's down there?

1270
00:56:53,243 --> 00:56:54,786
This is amazing.

1271
00:57:02,836 --> 00:57:03,962
What the heck?

1272
00:57:04,045 --> 00:57:05,004
What the heck is this?

1273
00:57:06,589 --> 00:57:08,883
- What is it?
- What does it say?

1274
00:57:08,967 --> 00:57:14,097
There's a question mark drawn on the wall,
and there's a talisman stuck on the dot.

1275
00:57:14,180 --> 00:57:15,640
If you remove it, the challenge begins.

1276
00:57:16,641 --> 00:57:19,144
- What?
- Let me read it to you quietly.

1277
00:57:19,978 --> 00:57:23,314
"You have just discovered
the secret safe in the living area."

1278
00:57:23,940 --> 00:57:26,943
"This will be your chance
to add 200 million to the prize money."

1279
00:57:28,069 --> 00:57:30,029
"Please take the prize token."

1280
00:57:31,030 --> 00:57:32,282
- Prize token?
- Amazing.

1281
00:57:32,365 --> 00:57:34,159
- Come on, go get it.
- That's it?

1282
00:57:34,242 --> 00:57:38,079
"The prize token is protected
by a curse barrier."

1283
00:57:38,163 --> 00:57:39,998
"Avoid the curse and take the prize."

1284
00:57:40,081 --> 00:57:41,416
Avoid the curse?

1285
00:57:42,542 --> 00:57:44,794
"You may turn back if you do not wish
to take on the challenge."

1286
00:57:44,878 --> 00:57:46,921
Does it specify the number of players?

1287
00:57:47,005 --> 00:57:50,300
- I think they mean one by "you."
- Just one?

1288
00:57:50,383 --> 00:57:52,218
And if you fail--

1289
00:57:52,302 --> 00:57:54,471
It doesn't mention anything
about penalties.

1290
00:57:54,554 --> 00:57:55,722
It's not about penalties.

1291
00:57:55,805 --> 00:57:58,391
If we fail, we lose out on 200 million,
so this is really important.

1292
00:57:58,475 --> 00:57:59,601
What's more is that

1293
00:58:00,226 --> 00:58:01,978
this isn't just for us.
It's for the final prize.

1294
00:58:02,061 --> 00:58:04,647
- Right.
- So everyone benefits.

1295
00:58:04,731 --> 00:58:06,983
- I don't think we'll get a second chance.
- It's for everyone's sake.

1296
00:58:07,066 --> 00:58:10,153
So one person plays,
and it's up to them to succeed or fail.

1297
00:58:10,236 --> 00:58:11,821
- Yeah, since prize money's involved.
- Right.

1298
00:58:11,905 --> 00:58:13,990
- Okay, come up here first.
- Just hold off?

1299
00:58:14,073 --> 00:58:15,116
Can you take the key out?

1300
00:58:15,200 --> 00:58:16,367
Okay, hold on.

1301
00:58:16,451 --> 00:58:19,537
- Would we need to reassemble it?
- That would take way too much time.

1302
00:58:21,956 --> 00:58:23,416
If you can't, I'll just do it.

1303
00:58:23,500 --> 00:58:25,376
Never mind, it's going up.

1304
00:58:25,460 --> 00:58:26,794
Leave it exactly like that.

1305
00:58:26,878 --> 00:58:28,379
We don't wanna do it over.

1306
00:58:30,924 --> 00:58:33,384
- Just set it down like that.
- I'll leave it here.

1307
00:58:34,469 --> 00:58:37,931
I mean, we got something out of it.
That's a relief.

1308
00:58:38,014 --> 00:58:41,184
- Well done.
- It's not for personal gain.

1309
00:58:42,602 --> 00:58:43,436
It's for the prize money.

1310
00:58:43,520 --> 00:58:46,189
We need to have a rough idea
of what the challenge is

1311
00:58:46,272 --> 00:58:48,525
- to decide who to send.
- The way I see it,

1312
00:58:48,608 --> 00:58:50,401
it said a curse protects the prize token.

1313
00:58:50,485 --> 00:58:53,780
So I think that means,
if you fail to solve whatever it is,

1314
00:58:53,863 --> 00:58:56,199
- the 200 million disappears.
- Right, it's gone.

1315
00:58:56,282 --> 00:59:00,286
If you succeed,
then it gets added to the pot.

1316
00:59:00,370 --> 00:59:01,496
It could also be this.

1317
00:59:01,579 --> 00:59:03,915
The biggest benefit here

1318
00:59:03,998 --> 00:59:06,543
is the additional 200 million won
to the prize money.

1319
00:59:06,626 --> 00:59:10,630
But there's also the matter of
who actually solved it.

1320
00:59:10,713 --> 00:59:14,008
I think the person who succeeds
will get some sort of benefit as well.

1321
00:59:14,092 --> 00:59:16,886
For example, they'll get ten Pieces.

1322
00:59:16,970 --> 00:59:19,347
Or an advance notice of the final game.

1323
00:59:19,430 --> 00:59:22,141
Something like that must be in play.

1324
00:59:22,225 --> 00:59:24,102
- Did it mention any benefits?
- No.

1325
00:59:24,185 --> 00:59:25,228
- Not at all?
- Just…

1326
00:59:25,311 --> 00:59:27,897
<i>It's a common trait</i>
<i>of escape room players.</i>

1327
00:59:27,981 --> 00:59:29,983
No matter how many you walk in with,

1328
00:59:30,066 --> 00:59:31,651
you wanna be the one solving the puzzle.

1329
00:59:32,151 --> 00:59:34,112
Would you if you had the chance?

1330
00:59:34,195 --> 00:59:35,822
I definitely would.

1331
00:59:36,406 --> 00:59:39,284
- But it's a matter of trust.
- You have the justification.

1332
00:59:39,367 --> 00:59:41,828
- We found it together.
- Yeah, we found it first.

1333
00:59:41,911 --> 00:59:43,580
We found it together.

1334
00:59:43,663 --> 00:59:47,083
<i>Even before coming here, my thinking was,</i>

1335
00:59:47,166 --> 00:59:48,626
if there's a hidden stage of that kind,

1336
00:59:48,710 --> 00:59:50,545
I wouldn't be confident enough
to just go for it.

1337
00:59:50,628 --> 00:59:55,842
I have a feeling the game
won't be a typical battle of wits.

1338
00:59:55,925 --> 00:59:57,468
- Chances are it isn't.
- Yeah.

1339
00:59:57,552 --> 01:00:01,097
I don't think it'd be a bad idea
for Won-young to give it a go tonight.

1340
01:00:03,224 --> 01:00:05,018
- I agree that we should do it tonight.
- Yeah.

1341
01:00:05,101 --> 01:00:06,686
- I don't mind.
- Okay.

1342
01:00:06,769 --> 01:00:09,105
- I want Won-young to go.
- Me too.

1343
01:00:09,731 --> 01:00:12,942
If Won-young is confident about it,
I wanna back him up.

1344
01:00:13,026 --> 01:00:16,571
I also feel like
if he succeeds and comes back,

1345
01:00:16,654 --> 01:00:18,823
he'll become my ally.

1346
01:00:18,906 --> 01:00:20,575
<i>I gave him my trust,</i>

1347
01:00:20,658 --> 01:00:24,203
<i>so I thought he'd stand on my side</i>
<i>when I asked him to do so.</i>

1348
01:00:24,287 --> 01:00:27,373
- Okay.
- I'm not against it.

1349
01:00:27,457 --> 01:00:30,043
- Right.
- If everyone else agrees to it, then sure.

1350
01:00:30,126 --> 01:00:31,794
- All right.
- Let's give it a go.

1351
01:00:31,878 --> 01:00:32,962
- Okay.
- I'll be cheering you on.

1352
01:00:33,046 --> 01:00:34,672
He says he's confident.

1353
01:00:34,756 --> 01:00:36,424
- It's best to trust the volunteer.
- Let's trust him.

1354
01:00:36,507 --> 01:00:40,637
You really wanted
that 100 million won earlier too,

1355
01:00:40,720 --> 01:00:42,180
so I'm sure you'll do well.

1356
01:00:42,972 --> 01:00:44,182
I'll do my best.

1357
01:00:44,265 --> 01:00:47,352
If Won-young succeeds,

1358
01:00:47,435 --> 01:00:49,062
I'm sure he'll have an easier time

1359
01:00:49,145 --> 01:00:51,731
- in the games going forward.
- For sure.

1360
01:00:55,818 --> 01:00:56,653
- You got this.
- Good luck.

1361
01:00:56,736 --> 01:00:58,154
- Good luck.
- Good luck.

1362
01:01:00,948 --> 01:01:02,075
- Wish me luck.
- Good luck.

1363
01:01:02,158 --> 01:01:03,451
You look hilarious with that lamp.

1364
01:01:07,163 --> 01:01:08,539
No pressure.

1365
01:01:08,623 --> 01:01:10,083
Just relax.

1366
01:01:11,459 --> 01:01:12,293
I'll do my best.

1367
01:01:24,514 --> 01:01:26,808
REMOVE TO TAKE ON CHALLENGE

1368
01:01:41,030 --> 01:01:41,906
What the?

1369
01:01:56,003 --> 01:01:58,548
SECRET SAFE…
AVOID THE CURSE… CURSE BARRIER…

1370
01:02:06,806 --> 01:02:07,640
What the?

1371
01:02:10,059 --> 01:02:10,935
What is this?

1372
01:02:14,272 --> 01:02:15,732
This is pure horror!

1373
01:02:16,816 --> 01:02:17,817
- What?
- What did he say?

1374
01:02:17,900 --> 01:02:18,985
- He said "horror."
- Horror.

1375
01:02:19,068 --> 01:02:20,319
- What?
- Horror.

1376
01:02:20,403 --> 01:02:22,071
- He said, "It's horror."
- He said, "It's horror."

1377
01:02:25,324 --> 01:02:26,617
How do I get that out?

1378
01:02:34,834 --> 01:02:35,918
You can hear me, right?

1379
01:02:36,419 --> 01:02:37,545
- Yeah, we can.
- Yeah.

1380
01:02:38,546 --> 01:02:41,215
I need to reach out and grab the token.

1381
01:02:41,299 --> 01:02:43,259
- Grab what?
- The token.

1382
01:02:43,342 --> 01:02:46,512
But the way in has dirty weeds,

1383
01:02:46,596 --> 01:02:49,599
and behind them are two stone mounds
with bells on top of them.

1384
01:02:50,391 --> 01:02:52,894
And I have a feeling
I shouldn't let the bells fall.

1385
01:02:53,394 --> 01:02:54,812
What is he saying?

1386
01:02:54,896 --> 01:02:56,147
He's saying something, right?

1387
01:02:58,065 --> 01:02:58,941
How…

1388
01:03:00,026 --> 01:03:02,445
How am I supposed to take that out?

1389
01:03:03,780 --> 01:03:05,615
How do I avoid the bells?

1390
01:03:07,283 --> 01:03:08,576
Should I try digging?

1391
01:03:09,577 --> 01:03:10,995
Can I make some space?

1392
01:03:16,751 --> 01:03:18,294
"The prize token is protected
by a curse barrier."

1393
01:03:18,377 --> 01:03:20,296
"Avoid the curse and take the prize."

1394
01:03:20,963 --> 01:03:22,215
"Avoid the curse."

1395
01:03:23,466 --> 01:03:25,510
Do I just knock everything down
and take it?

1396
01:03:26,844 --> 01:03:28,930
Can I just quietly set the bells down?

1397
01:03:31,933 --> 01:03:33,392
Wait, will this shake the mound?

1398
01:03:35,895 --> 01:03:37,438
But do I have to do this? Hang on.

1399
01:03:37,522 --> 01:03:40,149
What triggers the curse,
the mounds or the bells?

1400
01:03:40,233 --> 01:03:41,400
"Curse barrier."

1401
01:03:42,443 --> 01:03:43,903
Can I hold them both?

1402
01:03:44,821 --> 01:03:47,323
Can I grab both at once,
using both hands?

1403
01:03:47,406 --> 01:03:48,783
Nope, I can't.

1404
01:03:49,742 --> 01:03:51,869
What if I lift it?
There's space at the top.

1405
01:03:51,953 --> 01:03:53,579
Maybe I can reach down.

1406
01:04:04,966 --> 01:04:06,259
Dang it, I can't reach it.

1407
01:04:06,926 --> 01:04:08,302
Damn it.

1408
01:04:10,680 --> 01:04:12,223
Can I move the mounds?

1409
01:04:15,434 --> 01:04:16,644
Damn it.

1410
01:04:18,354 --> 01:04:19,397
How's it going?

1411
01:04:22,358 --> 01:04:23,568
Come on.

1412
01:04:24,402 --> 01:04:25,611
Can I move them?

1413
01:04:25,695 --> 01:04:27,154
Is it okay
as long as the bells don't ring?

1414
01:04:31,826 --> 01:04:33,160
Dang, I'm f…

1415
01:04:34,120 --> 01:04:35,955
I'm feeling great.

1416
01:04:51,053 --> 01:04:52,513
- A door opened.
- I hear a weird sound.

1417
01:04:52,597 --> 01:04:54,265
- Did a door open?
- Did it close?

1418
01:04:54,348 --> 01:04:55,600
Did it open or close?

1419
01:04:57,810 --> 01:04:59,437
<i>Jung Won-young.</i>

1420
01:05:03,983 --> 01:05:04,817
Yes?

1421
01:05:08,279 --> 01:05:10,197
<i>Jung Won-young,</i>

1422
01:05:10,281 --> 01:05:14,911
<i>you have been cursed by the barrier</i>

1423
01:05:15,745 --> 01:05:19,081
<i>that was guarding the prize token.</i>

1424
01:05:19,165 --> 01:05:21,876
<i>Those who have been cursed</i>

1425
01:05:21,959 --> 01:05:24,670
<i>lose every Piece they hold</i>

1426
01:05:25,421 --> 01:05:28,674
<i>and will not be able to</i>

1427
01:05:28,758 --> 01:05:33,971
<i>acquire or use any Pieces</i>
<i>in </i>The Devil's Plan.

1428
01:05:36,390 --> 01:05:39,352
<i>To break the curse…</i>

1429
01:06:07,421 --> 01:06:08,839
What if this challenge

1430
01:06:09,423 --> 01:06:11,509
is like Knight's Tour?

1431
01:06:11,592 --> 01:06:14,303
Maybe he was given extra time
to begin a whole new game.

1432
01:06:14,387 --> 01:06:17,181
- That's a possibility.
- That'd explain why he's taking so long.

1433
01:06:17,765 --> 01:06:20,393
Or he might be getting a hint
about the next game.

1434
01:06:27,108 --> 01:06:29,151
Jang Dong-min and Oh Hyun-min.

1435
01:06:34,365 --> 01:06:35,741
What's going on?

1436
01:06:35,825 --> 01:06:36,659
There's the talisman.

1437
01:06:36,742 --> 01:06:38,244
Damn it.

1438
01:06:38,327 --> 01:06:40,413
He has something in his hand.

1439
01:06:41,372 --> 01:06:42,915
- What happened? Did you succeed?
- He brought it.

1440
01:06:43,624 --> 01:06:44,750
- Tell us.
- I think he did.

1441
01:06:44,834 --> 01:06:47,128
I guess you could call this
the greater failure.

1442
01:06:47,795 --> 01:06:49,338
- What?
- What's in your hand?

1443
01:06:49,422 --> 01:06:50,423
The prize token.

1444
01:06:50,506 --> 01:06:53,134
- Then why is it a fail?
- Then how is that a fail?

1445
01:06:53,217 --> 01:06:54,552
There was no way around it.

1446
01:06:54,635 --> 01:06:55,469
- What do you mean?
- What?

1447
01:06:55,553 --> 01:06:58,681
They were trying to make it really scary,

1448
01:06:58,764 --> 01:07:00,474
- but you just reach in and grab it.
- You mean these two?

1449
01:07:00,558 --> 01:07:02,226
- Then what did you fail?
- But the thing is,

1450
01:07:02,309 --> 01:07:03,811
the curse was unavoidable.

1451
01:07:03,894 --> 01:07:05,563
- You have to be cursed to retrieve them.
- Oh, I see.

1452
01:07:05,646 --> 01:07:07,148
- You've been cursed?
- What's the curse?

1453
01:07:07,231 --> 01:07:08,983
There were stone mounds on either side.

1454
01:07:09,066 --> 01:07:11,444
- And on top of them, there were bells.
- Okay.

1455
01:07:11,527 --> 01:07:13,487
- So only you were cursed?
- Yeah.

1456
01:07:13,571 --> 01:07:15,448
- The challenger gets cursed.
- Okay.

1457
01:07:15,531 --> 01:07:16,407
- So?
- Go on.

1458
01:07:16,490 --> 01:07:21,037
Basically, I had to risk getting cursed
to pursue the greater good.

1459
01:07:24,457 --> 01:07:26,876
- So it's the opposite.
- And you don't know what the curse is?

1460
01:07:26,959 --> 01:07:29,086
There's a condition.
The curse doesn't trigger automatically.

1461
01:07:29,170 --> 01:07:30,880
- What is it?
- If two players

1462
01:07:30,963 --> 01:07:32,548
aren't eliminated tomorrow,

1463
01:07:32,631 --> 01:07:33,758
I end up cursed.

1464
01:07:33,841 --> 01:07:34,842
- What?
- What?

1465
01:07:34,925 --> 01:07:36,635
- So we have to make that happen.
- In the main match?

1466
01:07:36,719 --> 01:07:38,095
- We'll have to.
- Yeah, in the main match.

1467
01:07:38,179 --> 01:07:42,808
And the curse would make it so that
I can never make it to the finals.

1468
01:07:42,892 --> 01:07:44,101
- What?
- What?

1469
01:07:45,144 --> 01:07:46,187
- That's brutal.
- What?

1470
01:07:46,270 --> 01:07:49,190
- I can never make it to the finals.
- No way.

1471
01:07:49,273 --> 01:07:50,608
- You can't reach the finals?
- No.

1472
01:07:50,691 --> 01:07:52,318
- It's a ban.
- I'm not being eliminated.

1473
01:07:52,401 --> 01:07:54,612
If two aren't eliminated tomorrow,
he won't make it to the finals.

1474
01:07:54,695 --> 01:07:57,156
Right, that's the curse.

1475
01:07:57,239 --> 01:08:00,993
Won-young, if others find out
about that condition of the curse,

1476
01:08:01,077 --> 01:08:03,412
- you'll be in trouble.
- They can't find out about that.

1477
01:08:03,496 --> 01:08:06,999
- They can't know what triggers it.
- The others can't find out.

1478
01:08:08,000 --> 01:08:10,669
Right, don't let them know
about your curse.

1479
01:08:10,753 --> 01:08:14,215
Did I just make sure I'll get cursed?

1480
01:08:14,298 --> 01:08:15,299
- No, you didn't.
- No.

1481
01:08:15,382 --> 01:08:18,135
We'll help you
by getting two people eliminated.

1482
01:08:18,219 --> 01:08:20,304
Let's just keep this to ourselves

1483
01:08:20,387 --> 01:08:22,765
- and get two people eliminated tomorrow.
- That'd be for the best.

1484
01:08:22,848 --> 01:08:24,934
But if that's the case,

1485
01:08:25,726 --> 01:08:27,520
regardless of who went inside,

1486
01:08:27,603 --> 01:08:29,814
- we made the decision together.
- Right.

1487
01:08:29,897 --> 01:08:32,650
- So we need to create an alliance
- Yeah, for sure.

1488
01:08:32,733 --> 01:08:35,111
- and protect that person.
- Yeah.

1489
01:08:35,694 --> 01:08:37,655
Our alliance should split into
three and four people,

1490
01:08:37,738 --> 01:08:40,199
but really, we're an alliance of seven.

1491
01:08:40,282 --> 01:08:42,118
But given what happened today,

1492
01:08:42,201 --> 01:08:44,370
we need the other guys
to think there's a division.

1493
01:08:44,453 --> 01:08:47,748
So the four of us can go to one room,

1494
01:08:47,832 --> 01:08:51,377
and the three of you
can go to another room.

1495
01:08:51,460 --> 01:08:56,132
But personally speaking,
if XITSUH asks to play together,

1496
01:08:56,215 --> 01:08:59,301
at the very least,
I have to help him in tomorrow's games.

1497
01:08:59,385 --> 01:09:01,762
- Then you guys should have three members.
- Right.

1498
01:09:01,846 --> 01:09:03,764
Is Jang-Oh not splitting up?

1499
01:09:03,848 --> 01:09:05,599
I don't think they can tomorrow,

1500
01:09:06,433 --> 01:09:07,852
especially if XITSUH is joining them.

1501
01:09:07,935 --> 01:09:10,020
So it'll be the three of you
and the four of us.

1502
01:09:10,771 --> 01:09:11,605
Then we're good.

1503
01:09:11,689 --> 01:09:13,732
If XITSUH agrees to join,

1504
01:09:13,816 --> 01:09:16,902
- then it'll be an alliance of eight.
- Yeah.

1505
01:09:16,986 --> 01:09:19,155
Then two of the remaining four
will need to be cut.

1506
01:09:19,238 --> 01:09:21,824
Who's left? Seok-jin, Hyun-gyu, JAE JAE…

1507
01:09:21,907 --> 01:09:23,617
- John.
- Right, John.

1508
01:09:25,035 --> 01:09:27,830
- Those are some tough players.
- They're no pushovers.

1509
01:09:27,913 --> 01:09:30,374
That's how we'll present ourselves
to them.

1510
01:09:30,457 --> 01:09:34,962
Whatever game comes up,
nobody would find it suspicious

1511
01:09:35,045 --> 01:09:38,507
to see Shindong and me talking.

1512
01:09:38,591 --> 01:09:41,969
So I'll pass along the orders to him.

1513
01:09:42,052 --> 01:09:43,220
- Okay.
- How's that?

1514
01:09:43,304 --> 01:09:44,555
Good strategy.

1515
01:09:45,389 --> 01:09:46,932
- Good job.
- Let's go to bed.

1516
01:09:47,016 --> 01:09:49,435
- Good work.
- Good job.

1517
01:09:50,102 --> 01:09:51,395
I shouldn't have volunteered.

1518
01:09:52,229 --> 01:09:54,398
- But it was courageous.
- It was cool of you to do that.

1519
01:09:54,481 --> 01:09:58,110
But what sort of disadvantage
prevents you from going to the finals?

1520
01:09:58,194 --> 01:10:01,488
- Some sort of benefit…
- They can make anything happen.

1521
01:10:01,572 --> 01:10:04,325
But it still has to make sense.

1522
01:10:14,001 --> 01:10:15,002
Won-young.

1523
01:10:17,838 --> 01:10:18,756
Won-young.

1524
01:10:19,506 --> 01:10:20,341
Yes?

1525
01:10:34,480 --> 01:10:35,606
You can't trick me.

1526
01:10:42,821 --> 01:10:44,865
I didn't let on in front of the others.

1527
01:10:47,076 --> 01:10:52,081
It'll just make things difficult
if you lie.

1528
01:12:30,763 --> 01:12:35,768
Subtitle translation by: Justin S. Kim
